WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:07.040
You've seen the title so you know this is leading&nbsp;
to a certain fractal. Actually it's an infinite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:12.160
family of fractals. And yeah, it'll be one of&nbsp;
those mind-bogglingly intricate shapes that has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:16.960
infinite detail no matter how far you zoom in. But&nbsp;
this is not really a video about generating some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:21.200
pretty picture for us to gawk at. Well, okay,&nbsp;
maybe that's part of it, but the real story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:25.520
here has a much more pragmatic starting point&nbsp;
than the story behind a lot of other fractals.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:28.160
And more than that, the final images that we get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:31.680
to will become a lot more meaningful&nbsp;
if we make an effort to understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:37.040
why given what they represent they kind of&nbsp;
have to look as complicated as they do, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:43.840
what this complexity reflects about an algorithm&nbsp;
that is used all over the place in engineering.

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:52.000
The starting point here will be to assume that&nbsp;
you have some kind of polynomial and that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:56.960
want to know when it equals zero. So for the one&nbsp;
graphed here you can visually see there are three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:01.120
different places where it crosses the x-axis, and&nbsp;
you can kind of eyeball what those values might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:06.240
be. We'd call those the roots of the polynomial.&nbsp;
But how do you actually compute them exactly?

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:10.320
Now, this is the kind of question where&nbsp;
if you're already bought into math maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:13.760
it's interesting enough in its own right to&nbsp;
move forward. But if you just pull someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:16.960
on the street aside and ask them this,&nbsp;
I mean they're already falling asleep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:22.560
because who cares? But the thing is this kind of&nbsp;
question comes up all the time in engineering.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:25.920
Where I'm personally most familiar&nbsp;
with equations like this popping up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:30.640
is in the setting of computer graphics, where&nbsp;
polynomials are just littered all over the place.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:34.640
So it's not uncommon that when you're figuring&nbsp;
out how a given pixel should be colored&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:38.320
that somehow involves solving an&nbsp;
equation that uses these polynomials.

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:43.760
Here let me give you one fun example. When&nbsp;
a computer renders text on the screen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:47.280
those fonts are typically not&nbsp;
defined using pixel values.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:52.480
They're defined as a bunch of polynomial curves,&nbsp;
known in the business as "Bézier curves",&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:57.680
and any of you who've messed around with vector&nbsp;
graphics, maybe in some design software, would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:03.280
be well familiar with these kinds of curves. But&nbsp;
to actually display one of them on the screen you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:08.080
need a way to tell each one of the pixels of your&nbsp;
screen whether it should be colored in or not.

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:12.880
These curves can be displayed either with some&nbsp;
kind of stroke width or, if they enclose a region,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:17.520
some kind of fill for that region. But if&nbsp;
you step back and you really think about it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:21.520
it's an interesting puzzle to figure out&nbsp;
how each one of the pixels knows whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:24.800
it should be colored in or not just&nbsp;
based on the pure mathematical curve.

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:29.600
I mean, take the case of stroke width. This&nbsp;
comes down to understanding how far away a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:34.960
given pixel is from this pure mathematical&nbsp;
curve, which itself is some platonic ideal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:39.760
it has zero width. You would think of it as&nbsp;
a parametric curve that has some parameter t.

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:44.720
Now one thing that you could do to figure out&nbsp;
this distance is to compute the distance between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:50.000
your pixel and a bunch of sample points on that&nbsp;
curve, then figure out the smallest. But that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:55.280
both inefficient and imprecise. Better is to get&nbsp;
a little mathematical and acknowledge that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.280 --> 00:03:00.560
distance to the curve at all the possible points&nbsp;
is itself some smooth function of the parameter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:05.360
and as it happens the square of that&nbsp;
distance will itself be a polynomial,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:06.880
which makes it pretty nice to deal with.

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:11.280
And if this were meant to be a full lesson on&nbsp;
rendering vector graphics we could expand all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:15.760
that out and embrace the mess. But right now&nbsp;
the only salient point that I want to highlight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:20.640
is that in principle this function whose&nbsp;
minimum you want to know is some polynomial.

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:24.480
Finding this minimum, and hence&nbsp;
determining how close the pixel is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:26.720
to the curve and whether it should get filled in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:31.760
is now just a classic calculus problem. What you&nbsp;
do is figure out the slope of this function graph,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:37.600
which is to say its derivative, again some&nbsp;
polynomial, and you ask when does that equal zero.

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:44.880
So to actually carry out this seemingly simple&nbsp;
task of just displaying a curve, wouldn't it be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:49.840
nice if you had a systematic and general way to&nbsp;
figure out when a given polynomial equals zero.

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:54.560
Of course, we could draw a hundred other&nbsp;
examples from a hundred other disciplines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:58.000
I just want you to keep in mind that&nbsp;
as we seek the roots of polynomials,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:01.520
even though we always display it in a&nbsp;
way that's cleanly abstracted away from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:07.200
the messiness of any real-world problem, the&nbsp;
task is hardly just an academic one. But again&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:10.320
ask yourself, how do you actually&nbsp;
compute one of those roots?

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:15.680
If whatever problem you're working&nbsp;
on leads you to a quadratic function,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:19.200
then happy days, you can use the quadratic&nbsp;
formula that we all know and love.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:24.400
And as a fun side note by the way, again&nbsp;
relevant to root-finding in computer graphics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:28.320
I once had a Pixar engineer give me the&nbsp;
estimate that considering how many lights&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:32.240
were used in some of the scenes for the movie&nbsp;
Coco, and given the nature of some of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:36.720
per-pixel calculations when polynomially&nbsp;
defined things like spheres are involved,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:41.840
the quadratic formula was easily used multiple&nbsp;
trillions of times in the production of that film.

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:48.080
When your problem leads you to a higher-order&nbsp;
polynomial things start to get trickier. For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:53.440
cubic polynomials, there is also a formula, which&nbsp;
Mathologer has done a wonderful video on. There's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:58.480
even a quartic formula, something that solves&nbsp;
degree-4 polynomials, although honestly that one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:02.960
is such a god-awful nightmare of a formula that&nbsp;
essentially no one actually uses it in practice.

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:08.080
But after that, and I find this one of the&nbsp;
most fascinating results in all of math,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:13.120
you cannot have an analogous formula to solve&nbsp;
polynomials that have a degree five or more.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:17.360
More specifically, for a pretty&nbsp;
extensive set of standard functions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:21.680
you can prove that there is no possible way&nbsp;
that you can combine those functions together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:26.480
that allows you to plug in the coefficients of&nbsp;
a quintic polynomial and always get out a root.

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:30.080
This is known as the unsolvability of the quintic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:33.760
which is a whole other can of worms we&nbsp;
can hopefully get into it some other time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:38.640
but in practice, it kind of doesn't matter&nbsp;
because we have algorithms to approximate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:42.480
solutions to these kinds of equations&nbsp;
with whatever level of precision you want.

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:47.440
A common one, and the main topic for&nbsp;
you and me today, is Newton's method.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:50.320
And yes, this is what will lead&nbsp;
us to the fractals, but I want you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:54.400
to pay attention to just how innocent and&nbsp;
benign the whole procedure seems at first.

00:05:55.360 --> 00:06:00.800
The algorithm begins with a random guess let's&nbsp;
call it x_0. Almost certainly the output of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:05.280
your polynomial at x_0 is not zero, so you&nbsp;
haven't found a solution, it's some other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:09.600
value visible as the height of this graph&nbsp;
at that point. So to improve the guess, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:15.280
idea is to ask when does a linear approximation&nbsp;
to the function around that value equal zero?

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:20.480
In other words if you were to draw a tangent line&nbsp;
to the graph at this point, when does that tangent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:26.320
line cross the x-axis? Now assuming this tangent&nbsp;
line is a decent approximation of the function&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.320 --> 00:06:31.120
in the loose vicinity of some true root, the place&nbsp;
where this approximation equals zero should take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:36.320
you closer to that true root. As long as you're&nbsp;
able to take a derivative of this function, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:40.960
with polynomials you'll always be able to do that,&nbsp;
you can concretely compute the slope of this line.

00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:45.520
So here's where the active viewers among you&nbsp;
might want to pause and ask how do you figure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.520 --> 00:06:49.360
out the difference between the current guess&nbsp;
and the improved guess. What is the size of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.360 --> 00:06:54.320
this step? One way to think of it is to consider&nbsp;
the fact that the slope of this tangent line,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.320 --> 00:06:59.600
its rise over run, looks like the height of&nbsp;
this graph divided by the length of that step.

00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:04.240
But on the other hand of course, the slope of the&nbsp;
tangent line is the derivative of the polynomial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.240 --> 00:07:09.760
at that point. If we rearrange this equation&nbsp;
here, this gives you a super concrete way that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.760 --> 00:07:16.000
you can compute that step size. So the next guess&nbsp;
which we might call x1, is the previous guess&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:21.840
adjusted by this step size. And after that you&nbsp;
can just repeat the process. You compute the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:27.360
value of this function and the slope at this new&nbsp;
guess which gives you a new linear approximation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:32.000
and then you make the next guess x2 wherever&nbsp;
that tangent line crosses the x-axis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:37.200
And then apply the same calculation to x2&nbsp;
and this gives you x3, and before too long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:43.840
you find yourself extremely close to a true root&nbsp;
pretty much as close as you could ever want to be

00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:48.000
It's always worth gut-checking that&nbsp;
a formula actually makes sense, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:52.720
in this case, hopefully it does. If p(x)&nbsp;
is large, meaning the graph is really high,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.720 --> 00:07:58.720
you need to take a bigger step to get down to&nbsp;
a root. But if P'(x) is also large meaning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.720 --> 00:08:03.120
the graph is quite steep, you should maybe&nbsp;
ease off on just how big you make that step.

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:09.040
As the name suggests this was a method that&nbsp;
newton used to solve polynomial expressions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.040 --> 00:08:12.560
but he sort of made it look a lot&nbsp;
more complicated than it needed to be,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.560 --> 00:08:16.800
and a fellow named Joesph Raphson published&nbsp;
a much simpler version, more like what you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:21.520
and I are looking at now, so you also often hear&nbsp;
this algorithm called the Newton-Raphson method.

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:26.640
These days it's a common topic in calculus&nbsp;
classes. One nice little exercise to try to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.640 --> 00:08:31.360
get a feel for it, by the way, is to try using&nbsp;
this method to approximate square roots by hand.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.040 --> 00:08:36.800
But what most calculus students&nbsp;
don't see, which is unfortunate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:40.080
is just how deep things can get&nbsp;
when you let yourself play around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:44.160
with this seemingly simple procedure and&nbsp;
start kind of picking at some of its scabs.

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:49.360
You see while newton's method works great&nbsp;
if you start near a root where it converges&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:53.840
really quickly, if your initial guess is far&nbsp;
from a root it can have a couple foibles.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:58.640
For example let's take the function we&nbsp;
were just looking at but shifted upward,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:00.720
and play the same game with the same initial guess

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:12.080
Notice how the sequence of new guesses that&nbsp;
we're getting kind of bounces around the local&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:16.720
minimum of this function sitting above the&nbsp;
x-axis. And this should kind of make sense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.720 --> 00:09:21.520
I mean a linear approximation of the function&nbsp;
around these values all the way to the right is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.520 --> 00:09:26.240
pretty much entirely unrelated to the nature of&nbsp;
the function around the one true root that it has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.240 --> 00:09:31.680
off to the left. So they're sort of giving you&nbsp;
no useful information about that true root.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.680 --> 00:09:36.640
It's only when this process just happens to throw&nbsp;
the new guess off far enough to the left by chance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:40.800
that the sequence of new guesses does anything&nbsp;
productive and actually approaches that true root.

00:09:42.560 --> 00:09:47.440
Where things get especially interesting is if&nbsp;
we ask about finding roots in the complex plane.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.240 --> 00:09:52.560
Even if a polynomial like the one shown&nbsp;
here has only a single real number root,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.560 --> 00:09:57.360
you'll always be able to factor this polynomial&nbsp;
into five terms like this if you allow these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.360 --> 00:10:02.080
roots to potentially be complex numbers. This&nbsp;
is the famous fundamental theorem of algebra.

00:10:02.880 --> 00:10:06.880
In the happy-go-lucky land of functions with&nbsp;
real number inputs and real number outputs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.880 --> 00:10:11.280
where you can picture the association between&nbsp;
